{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And when the layer of dew went up, etc.</b> When the sun shines, the dew that is upon the manna rises toward the sun, the way dew rises toward the sun — even if you fill a reed-tube of an egg with dew and seal its opening and set it in the sun, it rises on its own into the air. And our Rabbis interpreted that the dew rises from the earth into the air, and as the layer of dew ascended, the manna was revealed and they saw <b>and behold, on the face of the wilderness, etc.</b>" "<b>Fine.</b> A fine thing." "<b>And as hoarfrost.</b> Exposed, and there is nothing like it in Scripture. And one may say מְחֻסְפָּס — a term for a wrapper and דְּלוּסְקְמָא in the language of the Mishnah: when the layer of dew was removed, they saw that there was a fine thing מְחֻסְפָּס inside it, between the two layers of dew. And Onkelos translated it מְקַלַּף — a term for exposing the white." "<b>Like frost.</b> כְּפוֹר — יילי״דא בְּלַעַז, which is thin like גִּיר, ״כְּאַבְנֵי גִיר״ (Isaiah 27), and it is a kind of black dye, as we say concerning covering the blood: ״הַגִּיר וְהַזַּרְנִיךְ״ (Chullin 8). ״Thin like גִּיר, like a film upon the ground״ — it was thin like גִּיר and lay layered like ice upon the earth, and so is its meaning: דַּק כַּכְּפֹר — spread out, congealed and connected like a film. דַּק — טינב״ש בְּלַעַז, for it spread a thin film on top; ״וּכְגִיר״ which Onkelos translated is an addition beyond the language of the Hebrew and has no word in the verse."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {3 "Onkelos's Aramaic כגיר in the verse translation is his own addition; the Hebrew text reads only דק ככפר. Rashi explains his reference to גיר by citing Onkelos's expanded rendering."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:46.162312264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}